===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|drove}}, {{m|enm|drof}}, {{m|enm|draf}}, from {{inh|en|ang|drāf||action of driving; a driving out, expulsion; drove, herd, band; company, band; road along which cattle are driven}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*draibō||a drive, push, movement, drove}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰreybʰ-||to drive, push}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|drave}}, {{m|sco|dreef||drove, crowd}}, {{cog|nl|dreef||a walkway, wide road with trees, drove}}, {{cog|gmh|treip||a drove}}, {{cog|sv|drev||a drive, drove}}, {{cog|is|dreif||a scattering, distribution}}. More at {{l|en|drive}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A cattle [[drive]] or the [[herd]] being driven by it; thus, a [[number]] of [[cattle]] [[drive]]n to [[market]] or new [[pasture]]s.
# {{lb|en|figuratively|by extension|usually|in the plural}} A large number of people on the move.
#: {{coi|en|[[in droves|in '''droves''']]}}
# {{lb|en|collective}} A group of [[hare]]s.
# A [[road]] or [[track]] along which [[cattle]] are [[habitually]], used to be or coil be [[drive]]n; a [[droveway]].
# A narrow [[drain]] or [[channel]] used in the [[irrigation]] of [[land]].<ref>{{R:Simmonds Trade}}</ref>
# A broad [[chisel]] used to bring [[stone]] to a nearly [[smooth]] surface.
# The grooved surface of stone finished by the drove chisel.

